\begin{cases}
x \equiv 3 \pmod{4} \\
x \equiv 2 \pmod{5} \\
x \equiv 4 \pmod{7}
\end{cases}
\] We need to find the smallest positive integer \( x \) that satisfies all three congruences. One way to solve this system of congruences is to use the Chinese Remainder Theorem. We can use the method of successive substitutions to find a solution. First, let's find a number that satisfies the first two congruences. We can write \( x = 4k + 3 \). Substituting this into the second congruence, we get \( 4k + 3 \equiv 2 \pmod{5} \), which simplifies to \( 4k \equiv -1 \pmod{5} \) or \( 4k \equiv 4 \pmod{5} \). Dividing by 4 (which is valid since 4 and 5 are coprime), we get \( k \equiv 1 \pmod{5} \). So, \( k = 5m + 1 \) for some integer \( m \). Substituting this back into the expression for \( x \), we get \( x = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 7 \). Now we need to satisfy the third congruence: \( 20m + 7 \equiv 4 \pmod{7} \). Simplifying this, we get \( 20m \equiv -3 \pmod{7} \) or \( 20m \equiv 4 \pmod{7} \). Since \( 20 \equiv 6 \pmod{7} \), this becomes \( 6m \equiv 4 \pmod{7} \). To solve for \( m \), we need the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6, since \( 6 \cdot 6 = 36 \equiv 1 \pmod{7} \). So, multiplying both sides by 6, we get \( m \equiv 24 \equiv 3 \pmod{7} \). Thus, \( m = 7n + 3 \) for some integer \( n \). Substituting this back into the expression for \( x \), we get \( x = 20(7n + 3) + 7 = 140n + 67 \). The smallest positive integer \( x \) is when \( n = 0 \), so \( x = 67 \). </think> <answer> 67 </answer>

